---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `n         binary    linear    sort
8         4         8         28
16        5         16        120
32        6         32        496
1000000   20        1000000   499999500000`;

const Figure2 = `Double the list.

  binary search    one more step
  linear search    twice the steps
  selection sort   about four times the steps`;

const Figure3 = `n     doubling
8     256
16    65536
32    4294967296
64    18446744073709551616`;

const Task1 = `function halvings(n) {
  let steps = 0;
  let left = n;

  while (left > 0) {
    steps = steps + 1;
    left = Math.floor(left / 2);
  }

  return steps;
}

function pairs(n) {
  return (n * (n - 1)) / 2;
}

let sizes = [8, 16, 32, 64];

console.log("n\\tbinary\\tlinear\\tsort");

for (let i = 0; i < sizes.length; i++) {
  let n = sizes[i];
  console.log(n + "\\t" + halvings(n) + "\\t" + n + "\\t" + pairs(n));
}
`;

const Task2 = `// Add this procedure and this extra column.

function doubling(n) {
  let total = 1;

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    total = total * 2;
  }

  return total;
}

console.log("");
console.log("n\\tdoubling");

for (let i = 0; i < sizes.length; i++) {
  let n = sizes[i];
  console.log(n + "\\t" + doubling(n));
}
`;

const Task3 = `// A computer does one billion steps per second.
//
// For each of these, work out roughly how long the job takes
//   and write the answer in a comment:
//
//   1. Linear search through 1 billion records.
//   2. Binary search through 1 billion sorted records.
//   3. Selection sort on 1 million records.
//   4. A doubling algorithm on an input of 64.
//
// One of these four does not finish in your lifetime.
//   Say which, and by how much you missed.
`;

const ExampleOutput = `n	binary	linear	sort
8	4	8	28
16	5	16	120
32	6	32	496
64	7	64	2016`;

const NotationJs = `for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
  for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) {
    // one step
  }
}`;

const NotationPseudo = `i ← 1
REPEAT UNTIL(i > n)
{
  j ← 1
  REPEAT UNTIL(j > n)
  {
    <one step>
    j ← j + 1
  }
  i ← i + 1
}`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 2.13: Algorithmic Efficiency</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 2.13</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Algorithmic Efficiency</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>Counting Steps, Not Seconds</p>
              <p>What Happens When the Input Grows</p>
              <p>Reasonable and Unreasonable Time</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Why Not Just Time It</h2>
              <p>A fast computer makes a bad algorithm look fine.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Until the input gets bigger.</p>
              <p class="fragment">So we count <b>steps as the input grows</b>, not seconds.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Everything You Have Written This Unit</h2>
              <Code code={Figure1} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Steps for a List of n" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Look at the bottom row.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Shape Matters, Not the Number</h2>
              <Code code={Figure2} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Double the Input" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">One more step. Twice. Four times.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Those three answers are the whole subject.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Where the Shapes Come From</h2>
              <p><b>Halving</b> the problem each pass &rarr; one more step per doubling.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>One loop</b> over the list &rarr; steps grow with <code>n</code>.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>A loop inside a loop</b> &rarr; steps grow with <code>n</code> squared.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Then There Is This</h2>
              <Code code={Figure3} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Doubling" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Every <code>+1</code> to the input <b>doubles</b> the work.</p>
              <p class="fragment">At n = 64, a billion steps a second takes <b>585 years</b>.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Two Categories</h2>
              <p><b>Reasonable time:</b> steps grow like <code>n</code>, <code>n</code> squared, <code>n</code> cubed &mdash; a power of the input.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>Unreasonable time:</b> steps grow by doubling, or faster.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Read That Boundary Carefully</h2>
              <p><code>n</code> cubed on a million items is a very long time.</p>
              <p class="fragment">It is still called reasonable.</p>
              <p class="fragment">The line is not about being fast. It is about whether a bigger computer can ever save you.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Why That Line Is Drawn There</h2>
              <p>Reasonable: buy a machine 1000&times; faster, solve a much bigger problem.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Unreasonable: buy a machine 1000&times; faster, and handle about <b>ten more items</b>.</p>
              <p class="fragment">That is the difference. Hardware cannot rescue you.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Measuring an algorithm by counting steps as the input grows</li>
                <li>Recognizing the halving, linear, and squared shapes in code</li>
                <li>Distinguishing reasonable from unreasonable running time</li>
                <li>Explaining why a faster computer does not fix an unreasonable algorithm</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Efficiency</dt>
                  <dd>How the number of steps grows as the input grows.</dd>
                  <dt>Reasonable time</dt>
                  <dd>Steps grow like a power of the input &mdash; <code>n</code>, <code>n</code> squared, and so on.</dd>
                  <dt>Unreasonable time</dt>
                  <dd>Steps grow by doubling or faster as the input grows by one.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            Create a new <b>JavaScript program</b> named{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">2-13-efficiency</b>.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Build the Table</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Write the code below and run it.</li>
            <li>
              Look down each column as <code>n</code> doubles.{" "}
              <b>Write in a comment what happens to each one</b>, in words rather
              than numbers.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <Code code={Task1} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Efficiency" class="mt-3" />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Add the Fourth Shape</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Add the extra procedure and column, and run it again.</li>
            <li>
              <b>In a comment:</b> the sort column and the doubling column both
              look big at n = 8. Say which one you would rather be stuck with at
              n = 1000, and why the answer is obvious once you look at n = 64.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <Code code={Task2} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Efficiency" class="mt-3" />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Put a Time on It</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Fill in the comment block.</li>
          </ul>
          <Code code={Task3} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Efficiency" class="mt-3" />

          <h5 class="mt-4">Challenge (optional)</h5>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Print <code>doubling(64)</code> and compare the last few digits
              against the value on the slide. <b>They do not match.</b> Explain
              what happened, using what you know from Activity 1.2 about how many
              bits a number gets.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Exam Notation -->
        <section id="exam-notation">
          <h2>Same Idea, Exam Notation</h2>

          <div class="row g-3 my-4">
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <Code
                code={NotationJs}
                frame="code"
                lang="javascript"
                title="JavaScript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <Code
                code={NotationPseudo}
                frame="code"
                lang="txt"
                title="Exam Notation"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            <b>Counting steps is an exam skill, and it usually arrives as a
            nested loop like this one.</b> The question is almost always the
            same: how many times does the inner statement run?
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <b>Read the bounds, not the shape.</b> Two nested loops that each
              run <code>n</code> times give <code>n</code> squared steps. Two
              nested loops where the inner one runs 3 times give <code>3n</code>{" "}
              &mdash; which grows like <code>n</code>, not like <code>n</code>{" "}
              squared.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b><code>REPEAT n TIMES</code> gives you no counter.</b> When you
              need the index, you keep it yourself &mdash; which is why the
              notation on the right is longer than the JavaScript on the left.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            The written-response section asks a version of this about your own
            code: <b>state how many times the body of your first loop
            executes.</b> Same skill, your program instead of theirs.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Task 1 should produce exactly this.</p>

          <Code
            code={ExampleOutput}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            title="Example Output"
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            The columns are separated by tabs, so they may line up differently
            depending on your screen. The numbers are what matter.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>An algorithm taking <code>n</code> cubed steps is called reasonable. In your own words, why is that a sensible name for something that slow?</li>
            <li>Your computer gets a thousand times faster overnight. Which of the four columns benefits least, and why?</li>
            <li>Binary search beats linear search at every size in your table except the very smallest. Give a reason someone might still write the linear one.</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit the required files to the appropriate dropbox.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
